 \subsection{Decisions (post hoc)}
 \label{sec:decisions}
-Does the distinction matter when choosing an action? We compare two policies on the realized costs, each choosing among all nine options including no action (Table~\ref{tab:decisions}). Policy E takes the option with the lowest posterior-mean window energy. Policy C takes E's choice only if the posterior is at least 95\% confident that it lowers the energy by more than a margin $\delta$ (a fraction of the climatological standard deviation of $D_k$), and otherwise does nothing. Acting on the posterior mean gives the lower average regret. Requiring confidence never made an instance worse than doing nothing, where E did so in 0.5\% of instances at 2~LT and 4.5\% at 3~LT, but it forgoes gains: at 3~LT C acts in 70.5\% of instances and its regret is more than twice E's. Raising the margin to 0.1 of the climatological standard deviation leaves C unchanged at 2~LT and changes it little at 3~LT (acting 70.5\% to 66.5\%), so the confident effects are not concentrated near zero. These are results for these 200 instances; no harm in this panel is not a guarantee of none. The best of the nine options is confident for 65.0\% of instances at 2~LT and 20.5\% at 3~LT, and every confident choice is right.
